tae Joon is doing his best to save the  planet or at least part of it this pond  tae Joon is learning about environmental  preservation and sustainability on the  agenda today how frogs survive in ponds  this is the green school on the  Indonesian island of Bali the bamboo  paradise is located around a quarter of  an hour south of the small city of ubud  there's no central school building  instead individual classrooms are spread  across an eight hectare campus some of  the lessons for instance in green  studies take place outdoors I told them  like this is my pond I want them to fix  it up and I gave them sort of some  demands we've been studying the pond for  two weeks and we learned that the frogs  are dying in here because there's no  escape routes this gravel will allow the  frogs to reach land solar panels provide  eighty percent of the school's power the  rest comes from a bio gas generator that  uses composting toilets along with  ecology related subjects the school also  offers standard courses in math grammar  philosophy in physics the students come  from almost 40 different countries the  curriculum is internationally certified  right now in science class we're doing a  physics sort of a lab / which I call it  design engineering Pratt yes we're doing  a whole project where we have to build a  roller coaster and made out of rotten  and Bamber they have time and space to  be creative with the bamboo with the  return with each other and the whole  group dynamic piece it's just a much  more thematic approach to teaching than  you would get in a  ah school after recess it's time for  math but class begins with in israeli  folk dance there's so many kinesthetic  aspects that i can teach through the  photons moving forward moving backwards  keeping a beat stepping to the right  step into the left all amazing skills  which are for our human body that's a  sustainable education it's an education  for human beings not for universities  tuition costs around 10,000 euros a year  Balinese families can't afford that so  the green school remains in oasis for  the children of wealthy foreigners still  maybe one day they will share the  ecological knowledge they learned here  back in their home countries
 

